time now for morning rounds our look at  the medical news of the week we begin  with a study that looked at the safety  of drugs and therapies that are  classified as novel these are drugs and  products that are considered by the fda  to be innovative medications that either  serve unmet medical needs or  significantly help to advance patient  care and health the study looked at  approvals from 2001 to 2010 examining  222 drugs 71 of these about a third  ended up producing some sort of post  market safety incident it took an  average of around four years from the  time of approval to the first incident  being reported CBS new chief medical  correspondent dr. Jon LaPook and CBS  News contributor dr. Tara and a ruler  are here to talk to us about this John  to start with what type of actions were  taken and and when were the safety  concerns what were the safety concerns  with these drugs well first of all Tara  and I were both impressed at the  beginning of this just for perspective  beginning of the article they said that  most pivotal trials that form the basis  for these approvals involved fewer than  a thousand people for six months or less  which is this it's not very much not  very long so you would say you can see  why you it's so important to have the  post-marketing surveillance right keep  looking at after it gets approved of the  problems that they had there were three  that were actually withdrawn for the  market two were for cardiovascular  problems one was for a neurological  problem there was 61 black boxes you  know what that is when they put the  black box warning it says you know  there's really a problem and then there  were 59 safety communications in Tara  with the drugs that they looked at were  there any trends when it came to safety  there were a few trends they found that  biologic drugs so those that are made  from living cells as opposed to  chemicals seem to have more of these  incidents in addition drugs that were  designed for psychiatric diseases or  disorders and then in terms of approval  and how they were approved those drugs  that were nearing the end of meeting  their deadline for approval tended to  have more incidents as well as drugs  that were under the accelerated approval  track and when we talk about that we're  saying for example when you're looking  at trials or research and you're  typically approving a drug you might  look for did it improve survival when  it's in the accelerated track you're  looking at what we call  surrogate endpoints meaning did it  shrink a tumor size as opposed it did it  improve survival so in all of these four  areas that's where we saw more of the  incidents II John I mean there's this  ongoing debate John about how do you how  do you balance the whole idea of getting  these drugs in the hands of people  quickly versus you know ensuring safety  standards so where does the medical  community come down on this it is really  tough right on the one hand you end get  that drug soon especially if you have a  problem that it could treat on the other  hand you want to be safe and this has  been a real yin-yang and a lot of  friction in the medical community about  this the US Government Accountability  Office had a report about 18 months ago  and today I spoke to them just recently  if they feel the same way that they  think that if you have a process where  something's being accelerated in terms  of how quickly there's a flame lit under  it it's getting approved a little more  quickly than normal they think that then  post-market after it's approved you  should be looking at it more closely and  just to see you know anything going on  the FDA says look we have we look at  everything closely yeah and so we're not  they we're not looking at that  particularly more closely than anything  else so it's really really tough I mean  I think that dr. Scott Gottlieb the new  head of the FDA is going to have you  know a lot of issues on his hand trying  to figure out do you